Hello,
I am a student working on estimating the deer population of a large state park. I completed a 5 night road based distance sampling survey using spotlights and laser rangefinders on 32 transects. I used only dirt roads with low traffic and without roadside grazing areas for safety reasons and to potentially reduce road bias. To help alleviate the bias of using roads, I took cover type data for the sightings. I plan to find the density of deer in each cover type using distance sampling and extrapolate to the entire park.

I had a few questions about putting the data into DISTANCE.
Since my survey was 5 nights long should I do analysis for each night and average the results or combine all of the sighting data?
Also, in the results, why do the graphs show probability vs. sighting distance. From the YouTube videos, I thought that the graphs for this process were supposed to show the number of deer sightings vs distance and probability was calculated from this graph. and used in the equation N=(n/P)/(a/A)
My transects run through multiple cover types. This has complicated analysis  since I am somewhat confused on how to enter this into DISTANCE. Currently I have each cover type entered as a region and my area is the total area of that  cover type in the park as determined with ArcGIS. Should I enter all of the transects for each cover region category? Currently, I have estimated the percent cover type for each transect and I have multiplied this by each transect length. This makes it so that the transect lengths in each cover region are the percent of the transect that matched the cover type. Does that seem reasonable?
I noticed that changes in transect length do not affect the probability graph. Would it be easier for me to use the "p" value from the results and calculate N=(n/P)/(a/A) by hand for each cover region since the cover type data complicates Distance data entry?
Thank you,
-Jesse Morrison

